@startuml

header "Send message flow with Hybrid Broker (Phase 1+2: Fixed Topic Exchanges)"
footer "Author: Tuzaku | Topology: chat.public / chat.groups / chat.user-updates"

actor User1
participant "WebApp 1\n(Client)" as WebApp1
participant "Spring Boot\nInstance 1" as App1
participant "SimpleBroker\n(In-Memory)" as SimpleBroker
participant "RabbitMQ\nSubscription\nInterceptor" as SubInterceptor
participant "CustomRabbitMQ\nBrokerHandler" as RabbitHandler
participant "RabbitMQ\nBroker" as RabbitMQ
participant "DynamicRabbitMQ\nListener" as RabbitListener
participant "Spring Boot\nInstance 2" as App2
participant "DynamicRabbitMQ\nListener 2" as RabbitListener2
participant "SimpleBroker 2\n(In-Memory)" as SimpleBroker2
participant "WebApp 2\n(Client)" as WebApp2
database PostgreSQL

== Instance Startup (once per backend instance) ==

note over App1, RabbitMQ
  Fixed topology (declared at startup):
  - Topic exchanges: chat.public, chat.groups, chat.user-updates
  - One durable queue per instance: ws.instance-1.inbound
  - One consumer (DynamicRabbitMQListener) per instance queue
  Exchanges are declared once; no per-destination exchange creation on publish/subscribe.
end note

App1 -> RabbitHandler: @PostConstruct init()
RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: declareExchange(chat.public)
RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: declareExchange(chat.groups)
RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: declareExchange(chat.user-updates)
RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: declareQueue(ws.instance-1.inbound)
RabbitHandler -> RabbitListener: startListening(ws.instance-1.inbound)
RabbitListener -> RabbitMQ: Start consuming from ws.instance-1.inbound

== STOMP SUBSCRIBE (Hybrid Approach) ==

note over WebApp1, RabbitMQ
  Subscription is registered in BOTH SimpleBroker (local) and RabbitMQ (cross-instance).
  RabbitMQ sync adds/removes **bindings** on the per-instance inbound queue.
  No per-destination queue is created anymore.
end note

WebApp1 -> App1: STOMP SUBSCRIBE to /topic/public\n(subscriptionId=sub-1)
App1 -> SubInterceptor: preSend() - SUBSCRIBE
SubInterceptor -> RabbitHandler: handleSubscribe(sessionId, sub-1, "/topic/public")
RabbitHandler -> RabbitHandler: sessionSubscriptions[sessionId][sub-1] = "/topic/public"
RabbitHandler -> RabbitHandler: destinationSubscriptionCount["/topic/public"] = 1

alt First local subscription to this destination
  note right of RabbitHandler
    Only add binding when ref-count reaches 1.
    Route: /topic/public -> chat.public / routing key "public"
  end note
  RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: bind ws.instance-1.inbound\nto chat.public with key "public"
end

SubInterceptor -> App1: Allow SUBSCRIBE
App1 -> SimpleBroker: Register subscription (local)
SimpleBroker -> SimpleBroker: Add client to /topic/public subscribers
App1 -> WebApp1: Subscription confirmed

note over WebApp2, RabbitMQ
  Group chat uses the same binding model on chat.groups.
  Example: /topic/group.42 -> routing key "group.42"
end note

WebApp2 -> App2: STOMP SUBSCRIBE to /topic/group.42\n(subscriptionId=sub-2)
App2 -> SubInterceptor: preSend() - SUBSCRIBE
SubInterceptor -> App2: validateSubscription() - user is group member
SubInterceptor -> RabbitHandler: handleSubscribe(sessionId, sub-2, "/topic/group.42")

alt First local subscription to /topic/group.42 on Instance 2
  RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: bind ws.instance-2.inbound\nto chat.groups with key "group.42"
end

App2 -> SimpleBroker2: Register subscription (local)
App2 -> WebApp2: Subscription confirmed

== STOMP UNSUBSCRIBE / DISCONNECT ==

WebApp1 -> App1: STOMP UNSUBSCRIBE (subscriptionId=sub-1)\n(destination header is absent)
App1 -> SubInterceptor: preSend() - UNSUBSCRIBE
SubInterceptor -> RabbitHandler: handleUnsubscribe(sessionId, sub-1)
RabbitHandler -> RabbitHandler: lookup destination from sessionSubscriptions
RabbitHandler -> RabbitHandler: destinationSubscriptionCount["/topic/public"] = 0

alt Last local subscription to this destination
  RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: remove binding\nws.instance-1.inbound -> chat.public / "public"
end

note over RabbitHandler
  On DISCONNECT or SessionDisconnectEvent, all tracked subscriptions for the session are cleaned up the same way.
end note

== Send Public Message (Hybrid Approach) ==

User1 -> WebApp1: Type and send message
WebApp1 -> App1: STOMP SEND to /app/chat.send
App1 -> App1: @MessageMapping("/chat.send")
App1 -> PostgreSQL: Save message
PostgreSQL -> App1: Saved message
App1 -> App1: Create MessageResponse

note over App1
  Message is sent to BOTH:
  1. SimpleBroker (local subscribers on this instance)
  2. RabbitMQ (cross-instance via fixed topic exchange)
end note

App1 -> SimpleBroker: @SendTo("/topic/public") - MessageResponse
SimpleBroker -> WebApp1: Broadcast MessageResponse (local clients)

App1 -> RabbitHandler: publishToRabbitMQ("/topic/public", MessageResponse)
RabbitHandler -> RabbitHandler: route -> chat.public / "public"
RabbitHandler -> RabbitHandler: headers:\nsource-instance-id=instance-1\nstomp-destination=/topic/public
RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: publish chat.public, key "public"

note right of RabbitMQ
  Topic exchange routes only to queues with a matching binding.
  Instance 2 receives only if it has bound ws.instance-2.inbound to "public".
end note

RabbitMQ -> RabbitListener: Message on ws.instance-1.inbound
RabbitListener -> RabbitListener: source-instance-id = instance-1\n(current = instance-1) -> skip
note right of RabbitListener
  Same-instance echo is skipped to avoid duplicate delivery.
end note

RabbitMQ -> RabbitListener2: Message on ws.instance-2.inbound
RabbitListener2 -> RabbitListener2: source-instance-id = instance-1\n(current = instance-2) -> forward
RabbitListener2 -> RabbitListener2: read stomp-destination header\n-> /topic/public
RabbitListener2 -> RabbitListener2: Deserialize payload
RabbitListener2 -> SimpleBroker2: convertAndSend("/topic/public", payload)
SimpleBroker2 -> WebApp2: Broadcast MessageResponse (Instance 2 clients)

== Send Group Message (Hybrid Approach) ==

actor User2
note over User1, User2
  User1 is connected to Instance 1 and is a member of group 42.
  User2 is connected to Instance 2 and subscribed to /topic/group.42.
  Only group members can subscribe (validated by WebSocketSecurityChannelInterceptor).
end note

User1 -> WebApp1: Type and send group message
WebApp1 -> App1: STOMP SEND to /app/group.send\n(groupId=42)
App1 -> App1: @MessageMapping("/group.send")
App1 -> App1: validateGroup() - user is member
App1 -> PostgreSQL: saveGroupMessage(group, user, content)
PostgreSQL -> App1: Saved message
App1 -> App1: Create MessageResponse\ndestination = /topic/group.42

note over App1
  Group message is sent to BOTH:
  1. SimpleBroker (local subscribers on this instance)
  2. RabbitMQ via chat.groups (cross-instance)
  Unlike public chat, group send uses convertAndSend() instead of @SendTo.
end note

App1 -> SimpleBroker: convertAndSend("/topic/group.42", MessageResponse)
SimpleBroker -> WebApp1: Broadcast MessageResponse\n(local group subscribers on Instance 1)

App1 -> RabbitHandler: publishToRabbitMQ("/topic/group.42", MessageResponse)
RabbitHandler -> RabbitHandler: route -> chat.groups / "group.42"
RabbitHandler -> RabbitHandler: headers:\nsource-instance-id=instance-1\nstomp-destination=/topic/group.42
RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: publish chat.groups, key "group.42"

note right of RabbitMQ
  Topic exchange routes only to instances that have bound their inbound queue to "group.42".
  Instance 3 with no local subscribers to group 42 receives nothing.
end note

RabbitMQ -> RabbitListener: Message on ws.instance-1.inbound
RabbitListener -> RabbitListener: source-instance-id = instance-1\n(current = instance-1) -> skip

RabbitMQ -> RabbitListener2: Message on ws.instance-2.inbound
RabbitListener2 -> RabbitListener2: source-instance-id = instance-1\n(current = instance-2) -> forward
RabbitListener2 -> RabbitListener2: read stomp-destination header\n-> /topic/group.42
RabbitListener2 -> RabbitListener2: Deserialize payload
RabbitListener2 -> SimpleBroker2: convertAndSend("/topic/group.42", payload)
SimpleBroker2 -> WebApp2: Broadcast MessageResponse (Instance 2 clients)
WebApp2 -> User2: Display group message

== Async Group Summary Update (Sidebar) ==

participant "GroupSummaryUpdate\nPublisher (@Async)" as SummaryPublisher

note over App1, SummaryPublisher
  After a non-system group message is saved, the controller triggers an async sidebar refresh for every group member (even if they are not currently viewing this group chat).
end note

App1 -> SummaryPublisher: publishToGroupMembers(groupId, update) [async]
note right of SummaryPublisher
  Runs on groupSummaryUpdateExecutor. Does not block the group send handler.
end note

loop For each group member username
  SummaryPublisher -> SimpleBroker: convertAndSend("/topic/user.{username}.group-updates")
  SummaryPublisher -> RabbitHandler: publishToRabbitMQ("/topic/user.{username}.group-updates", update)
  RabbitHandler -> RabbitMQ: publish chat.user-updates,\nkey "user.{username}.group-updates"
end

== Key Points (Phase 1+2) ==

note right of SimpleBroker
  SimpleBroker handles:
  - Local WebSocket connections
  - Fast local message delivery
  - Per-instance subscription registry
end note

note right of RabbitMQ
  RabbitMQ handles:
  - Cross-instance message distribution
  - **3 fixed topic exchanges** (O(1) exchange count)
  - **1 inbound queue per instance** (O(instances) queue count)
  - **Dynamic bindings** per active destination
  - Topic routing by routing key
end note

note right of RabbitHandler
  CustomRabbitMQBrokerHandler:
  - Declares fixed exchanges + instance queue at startup
  - Tracks subscriptions by sessionId + subscriptionId
  - Adds/removes bindings on first/last local subscribe/unsubscribe
  - Publishes with routing key + stomp-destination header
end note

note right of RabbitListener
  DynamicRabbitMQListener:
  - **One consumer** per instance inbound queue
  - Filters self-delivery (source-instance-id)
  - Reads stomp-destination header to forward to correct STOMP topic
  - Forwards to SimpleBroker for local delivery
end note

@enduml
